---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { url } from '../data/site';

const settingsSnippet = `{
  "statusLine": {
    "type": "command",
    "command": "\${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/statusline.sh"
  }
}`;

const stdinJson = `{
  "model": { "id": "claude-opus-4-6", "display_name": "Opus 4.6" },
  "context_window": {
    "used_percentage": 38,
    "context_window_size": 200000,
    "current_usage": {
      "input_tokens": 1,
      "output_tokens": 9,
      "cache_creation_input_tokens": 653,
      "cache_read_input_tokens": 34682
    }
  },
  "cost": {
    "total_cost_usd": 0.42,
    "total_duration_ms": 720000
  }
}`;
---

<DocsLayout title="Status Line" sectionLabel="How It Works" activeSlug="status-line">

  <div class="toc">
    <h3>On This Page</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#overview">What It Is</a></li>
      <li><a href="#segments">Segment Breakdown</a></li>
      <li><a href="#how-it-works">How It Works Under the Hood</a></li>
      <li><a href="#data-sources">On-Disk Data Sources</a></li>
      <li><a href="#degradation">Graceful Degradation</a></li>
      <li><a href="#dashboard-sidecar">Dashboard Sidecar</a></li>
      <li><a href="#customization">Customization</a></li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Overview ── -->
  <section class="section" id="overview">
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h2>Status Line</h2>
      <p>While VibeCrew runs, a persistent status line at the bottom of your Claude Code terminal shows real-time session state. It updates after every assistant message at zero token cost &mdash; it's powered by a bash script, not the model.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What it is</strong>
            <p>A single ANSI-colored line rendered by <code>statusline.sh</code>. Claude Code pipes session JSON to the script's stdin on every assistant turn, and the script combines that with on-disk state files to produce a compact, color-coded status readout.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem it solves</strong>
            <p>Without the status line, you'd have to run <code>/status</code> repeatedly to check context usage, cost, and active feature. The status line gives you continuous visibility without spending tokens or interrupting your workflow.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <pre class="status-line-mock"><code>Opus 4.6 <span class="sl-ctx-green">▓▓▓▓░░░░░░ 38%</span> | <span class="sl-cost">$0.42</span> | <span class="sl-feat">auth-flow · Code</span> | <span class="sl-score">↑85+</span> | <span class="sl-dur">12m</span> | <span class="sl-compact">⟳0</span></code></pre>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Segments ── -->
  <section class="section" id="segments">
    <h3>Segment Breakdown</h3>
    <p>Seven segments, left to right:</p>

    <table class="solution-table status-line-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Example</th><th>What It Shows</th></tr></thead>
      <tr><td><strong>Model</strong></td><td><code>Opus 4.6</code></td><td>The Claude model powering the current session. Parsed from the session JSON that Claude Code pipes to the script on every turn.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Context Bar</strong></td><td><code>▓▓▓▓░░░░░░ 38%</code></td><td>A 10-character progress bar visualizing context window usage. Each <code>▓</code> block represents 10% of the 200k-token window. Color-coded: <span style="color:var(--color-green);">green</span> at &le;45%, <span style="color:var(--color-amber);">yellow</span> at 46&ndash;55%, and <span style="color:var(--color-red);">red</span> above 55%. This is the most critical indicator &mdash; when it turns red, consider running <code>/compact</code> or <code>/handoff</code> to free space before the model starts losing earlier context.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Session Cost</strong></td><td><code>$0.42</code></td><td>Cumulative API cost in USD for the current session. Helps you track spend in real time rather than waiting for a billing dashboard. The cost guardrail hook fires warnings at configurable thresholds independently, but the status line gives you a continuous readout.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Feature &middot; Phase</strong></td><td><code>auth-flow · Code</code></td><td>The active feature name (truncated to 16 characters) and its current phase in the Tier 2 cycle: Plan, Design, Code, Test, Review, or Docs. During Tier 1, this shows <code>foundation · Tier-1</code>. If no project is initialized, it displays <code>no project</code>. Read from <code>.vibecrew/state.json</code> on disk.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Vibe Score</strong></td><td><code>↑85+</code></td><td>The session's quality score (0&ndash;150) with a trend suffix: <code>+</code> means the score is improving across sessions, <code>-</code> means declining. Read from the latest score file in <code>.vibecrew/scores/</code>. When no score exists yet, displays <code>↑--</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Duration</strong></td><td><code>12m</code></td><td>Total elapsed session time in minutes. Shows <code>&lt;1m</code> for sessions under 60 seconds. Useful for gauging how long an autonomous run has been going.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Compactions</strong></td><td><code>⟳0</code></td><td>Number of <code>/compact</code> operations in the current session. Each compaction compresses the conversation to reclaim context window space. A rising number signals a long or context-heavy session. Read from <code>.vibecrew/sessions/</code>.</td></tr>
    </table>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── How It Works ── -->
  <section class="section" id="how-it-works">
    <h3>How It Works Under the Hood</h3>
    <p>Claude Code supports a <code>statusLine</code> setting in <code>settings.json</code> that points to a bash script. On every assistant turn, Claude Code pipes a JSON object to the script's stdin containing the current model, context window stats, cost, and timing data. The script parses this with <code>jq</code>, reads project state files from <code>.vibecrew/</code>, and outputs a single ANSI-colored line.</p>

    <h4>Configuration</h4>
    <p>The plugin's <code>settings.json</code> includes this entry, which Claude Code reads automatically:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={settingsSnippet} lang="json" label="settings.json" />

    <h4>stdin JSON Schema</h4>
    <p>This is the JSON object that Claude Code pipes to <code>statusline.sh</code> on every turn:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={stdinJson} lang="json" label="stdin payload" />
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Data Sources ── -->
  <section class="section" id="data-sources">
    <h3>On-Disk Data Sources</h3>
    <p>The script combines piped data with on-disk reads:</p>
    <table class="solution-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Data Source</th><th>What It Provides</th></tr></thead>
      <tr><td>stdin JSON</td><td>Model name, context percentage, session cost, elapsed time</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.vibecrew/state.json</code></td><td>Active feature name and current phase</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.vibecrew/scores/score-*.json</code></td><td>Latest Vibe Score and trend direction</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.vibecrew/sessions/session-*.json</code></td><td>Compaction count for the current session</td></tr>
    </table>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Graceful Degradation ── -->
  <section class="section" id="degradation">
    <h3>Graceful Degradation</h3>
    <p>The status line never breaks your terminal. If <code>jq</code> is missing, it displays just <code>VibeCrew</code>. If <code>.vibecrew/</code> doesn't exist, project-specific fields show placeholder values (<code>no project</code>, <code>↑--</code>, <code>⟳0</code>). The script always exits with code 0, regardless of parse errors.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Dashboard Sidecar ── -->
  <section class="section" id="dashboard-sidecar">
    <h3>Dashboard Sidecar</h3>
    <p>As a side effect, the status line script writes the raw session JSON to <code>.vibecrew/live-session.json</code> using an atomic move operation. The <a href={url('/dashboard.html')}>Vibe Dashboard</a> reads this file to display real-time session metrics in the browser without any additional polling or API calls.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ── Customization ── -->
  <section class="section" id="customization">
    <h3>Customization</h3>
    <p>The status line script is fully customizable. You can modify <code>statusline.sh</code> directly to change colors, add segments, or remove fields you don't need. Alternatively, point the <code>statusLine.command</code> setting to your own script &mdash; as long as it reads JSON from stdin and outputs a single line to stdout, Claude Code will display it.</p>
  </section>

</DocsLayout>

